1
00:00:00,440 --> 00:00:02,340
Few languages mix three different

2
00:00:02,360 --> 00:00:04,500
scripts in a single sentence.

3
00:00:04,520 --> 00:00:06,510
Japanese does it every day.

4
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:08,350
So how did that happen?

5
00:00:08,520 --> 00:00:10,340
Long before it was written down,

6
00:00:10,360 --> 00:00:11,940
Japanese was a living,

7
00:00:11,960 --> 00:00:13,540
spoken language, carried in

8
00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:16,220
conversation, song, and story.

9
00:00:16,240 --> 00:00:17,220
Its deeper origins

10
00:00:17,240 --> 00:00:18,700
are still debated.

11
00:00:18,720 --> 00:00:19,860
But for centuries,

12
00:00:19,880 --> 00:00:22,060
it had no script of its own.

13
00:00:22,080 --> 00:00:24,220
Writing came from across the sea.

14
00:00:24,240 --> 00:00:25,900
Through centuries of contact with

15
00:00:25,920 --> 00:00:27,700
China and the Korean Peninsula,

16
00:00:27,720 --> 00:00:29,020
Chinese characters reached

17
00:00:29,040 --> 00:00:30,620
the Japanese islands.

18
00:00:30,640 --> 00:00:32,420
These characters, kanji,

19
00:00:32,440 --> 00:00:34,060
were designed for Chinese,

20
00:00:34,080 --> 00:00:35,780
a very different language.

21
00:00:35,800 --> 00:00:38,420
So scribes in Japan adapted them:

22
00:00:38,440 --> 00:00:39,740
some for their meaning,

23
00:00:39,760 --> 00:00:41,900
others purely for their sound.

24
00:00:41,920 --> 00:00:43,220
Writing sounds with complex

25
00:00:43,240 --> 00:00:45,020
characters was slow.

26
00:00:45,040 --> 00:00:46,700
As scribes wrote faster,

27
00:00:46,720 --> 00:00:48,700
in flowing cursive brushwork,

28
00:00:48,720 --> 00:00:50,220
the shapes softened.

29
00:00:50,240 --> 00:00:51,060
From these cursive

30
00:00:51,080 --> 00:00:52,860
forms came hiragana.

31
00:00:52,880 --> 00:00:54,900
The kanji "an", for example,

32
00:00:54,920 --> 00:00:56,660
became the hiragana "a".

33
00:00:56,680 --> 00:00:58,660
Hiragana flourished in the poetry

34
00:00:58,680 --> 00:01:00,900
and literature of the Heian court.

35
00:01:00,920 --> 00:01:02,900
Meanwhile, Buddhist scholars needed

36
00:01:02,920 --> 00:01:05,300
quick notes beside Chinese texts.

37
00:01:05,320 --> 00:01:06,740
They took just one piece of a

38
00:01:06,760 --> 00:01:08,380
character, like the left side of

39
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:11,310
the kanji "i", and created katakana.

40
00:01:11,400 --> 00:01:14,100
Sharp, compact, precise.

41
00:01:14,120 --> 00:01:16,430
Today, all three work together.

42
00:01:16,520 --> 00:01:18,500
Kanji carry core meanings.

43
00:01:18,520 --> 00:01:20,180
Hiragana handle grammar.

44
00:01:20,200 --> 00:01:21,460
Katakana mark foreign

45
00:01:21,480 --> 00:01:23,220
words, and emphasis.

46
00:01:23,240 --> 00:01:24,580
Take this sentence.

47
00:01:24,600 --> 00:01:26,100
Watashi wa nihongo

48
00:01:26,120 --> 00:01:27,460
o benkyou shimasu.

49
00:01:27,480 --> 00:01:29,060
I study Japanese.

50
00:01:29,080 --> 00:01:31,060
Kanji form the core words.

51
00:01:31,080 --> 00:01:32,300
Hiragana link them,

52
00:01:32,320 --> 00:01:33,860
and complete the verb.

53
00:01:33,880 --> 00:01:35,220
Every line of Japanese

54
00:01:35,240 --> 00:01:36,660
carries more than a thousand

55
00:01:36,680 --> 00:01:38,780
years of history: borrowed,

56
00:01:38,800 --> 00:01:40,670
reshaped, and made its own.

57
00:01:40,760 --> 00:01:41,940
Three scripts.

58
00:01:41,960 --> 00:01:43,070
One language.

59
00:01:43,160 --> 00:01:44,750
Still written today.
